# Auth: phone verification by SMS code

Generated by `skies g auth:otp`. The cases in `e2e/` drive the booted app over HTTP with a capturing `ISmsSender`
layered over the real one, because the code is stored hashed and only ever leaves the system in an SMS.

## Behavior

- `POST /account/phone-code` (authenticated) sends a fresh 6-digit code to the given phone; it expires in 10 minutes.
- `POST /account/verify-phone` (authenticated) checks the code against the caller's latest active code. The right
  code marks the phone verified and completes registration; five wrong guesses lock the code.

Before `skies proof run` or `record`, review each mode against the AVP catalog and add criteria or a reviewed
exemption. Generated code cannot make that applicability decision on behalf of the consuming app.

## Failure modes

- FM-[verify] The code sent by SMS does not verify the caller's phone or complete their registration
- FM-[wrong-code] A wrong code verifies the phone, or burns the real code
- FM-[brute-force] The code can be guessed without limit: after five wrong codes the real one still verifies
- FM-[missing-fields] A request missing its phone or code fails as a server error, or a missing code spends one of the attempts

## Out of scope

- Delivery: the dev `ConsoleSmsSender` prints the message. A real provider is an `ISmsSender` the app registers.
- The throttle on requesting a code: the Account module's credential rate limit, proven with the auth spec.
